    "q-75300": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta .$",
      "worked_solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{0}{-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(0)}{-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{-{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-75300-a",
